  "key": "upbeat-turing-bEpCd",
  "name": "Fix Escape not closing the find bar",
  "description": "Escape only closed the Ctrl/Cmd+F find bar while its input was focused. Once focus left the input (jumping to a match, clicking a button, or clicking the diff), Escape did nothing. This makes Escape close find globally, mirroring the global open shortcut.",
  "harness": "claude-code",
  "branch": "claude/upbeat-turing-bEpCd",
  "baseRef": "b8b4af01cac6a76b4176ee64e23ac2a900643e98",
  "createdAt": 1780429462398,
  "updatedAt": 1780429462398,
  "fileCount": 1,
  "additions": 10,
  "deletions": 3,
  "stepCount": 1,
  "steps": [
    {
      "id": "step-1",
      "title": "Global Escape handler for find",
      "body": "The Ctrl/Cmd+F **open** shortcut is registered globally on `window`, but the **Escape-to-close** handler lived only on the find bar input (`FindBar.svelte`). As soon as focus left that input — pressing Enter to navigate matches, clicking a nav/close button, or clicking into the diff content — Escape no longer reached the input handler and the bar stayed open.\n\nThe fix extends the existing global keydown effect so Escape closes find whenever `find.open` is true, regardless of where focus is. `find` is now imported alongside the existing find actions.",
      "paths": [
        "apps/desktop/src/renderer/src/lib/components/DiffView.svelte"
      ],
      "callouts": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-c1",
          "file": "apps/desktop/src/renderer/src/lib/components/DiffView.svelte",
          "startLine": 282,
          "endLine": 286,
          "side": "new",
          "body": "New branch: Escape closes find from anywhere when it is open. When the input itself is focused its local handler fires first and closes find, so this branch then sees `find.open === false` and is a no-op — no double-close."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "files": [
    {
